BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antique Appraisals! Art Valuations w/ Bonhams Skinner
DTSTART:20261017T100000
DTEND:20261017T140000
DTSTAMP:20261017T141651Z
UID:fa02e5fc2806414160b516548c6c61@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Have you always wanted to know the value of treasures lying ar
 ound your home? For $10 per item\, or 3 items for $25\, Bonhams Skinner’
 s Robin Starr and Christopher Fox will assess your items. Register with Sc
 ott Davis\, sdavis@stjathenaeum.org or 820-745-1393. Starr is a fine art e
 xpert\, and Fox’s expertise includes decorative arts and furniture. Star
 r and a crew from Bonhams came to the Athenaeum in the spring of 2026 to d
 o an assessment of our art. Robin Starr is the General Manager of Bonhams 
 Skinner\, New England's oldest auction house\, with a distinguished histor
 y dating back to its founding in 1962. As Director of American & European 
 Paintings & Prints\, Robin Starr heads one of Skinner’s most important s
 pecialty departments. In this key role she oversees a staff of specialists
  who appraise and catalog prints\, paintings\, sculpture\, and works on pa
 per for a wide variety of Skinner clients\, including arts and cultural in
 stitutions\, estates\, major collectors\, and private individuals. She is 
 also widely regarded as one of Skinner’s most vibrant and animated aucti
 oneers\, bringing an enthusiastic spirit to the podium at Skinner’s Amer
 ican & European Paintings & Prints auctions\, held three times annually. S
 tarr’s area of specialization is in American paintings and prints from t
 he 19th and early 20th centuries. Outside her role at Skinner\, Starr is a
  major contributor to the success of Boston’s thriving arts and culture 
 community. She conducts numerous benefit appraisal days\, lectures\, and b
 enefit auctions throughout the year. Starr has lectured in art history at 
 various colleges throughout New England\, including as a Lecturer in Art H
 istory at Boston College in Chestnut Hill\, Massachusetts. Starr joined Sk
 inner in September of 1987 as a research and cataloguing assistant in the 
 American and European Paintings & Prints department. She received her M.A.
  in the History of Art\, specializing in early medieval art\, from William
 s College. As an undergraduate\, Starr combined an unusual double major in
  Art History and Physics through research in neutron activation autoradiog
 raphy and its application in art historical research at Bates College. Chr
 istopher D. Fox is Bonhams Skinner's Vice President and Director of Histor
 ic Arms & Militaria and Associate Director of American Furniture and Decor
 ative Arts. He specializes in military and civil arms used in 18th and 19t
 h century America\, and early American material culture\, including early 
 metalware\, ceramics\, and household items. Christopher has spent much of 
 his life immersed in early American material culture. He has authored nume
 rous articles and lectured on a wide variety of topics including 18th-cent
 ury British military firearms and powder horns\, early New York militia un
 iforms\, Seven Years' War period barrack room arrangements\, mid-18th cent
 ury English ceramics\, and the material culture associated with chocolate 
 in the 18th-century. He has assisted in exhibition development at local hi
 story museums. This event is ADA accessible.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:St. Johnsbury Athenaeum\, 1171 Main St.
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/antique-ap
 praisals-art-valuations-w-bonhams-skinner-05-10-2026-16-55-29
END:VEVENT
